      "rev_n_1_d91a0a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_d91a0a_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_d91a0a",
        "thought_text": "Scan the string from left to right to find the first index $i$ where $s[i] > s[i+1]$. Reversing the substring from $i$ to $i+1$ will swap these two characters, making the new string lexicographically smaller at the first differing position. This is the most direct and optimal greedy approach.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588391.0352082
      },
      "rev_n_2_ee9712_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_ee9712_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_ee9712",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible substrings $s[l..r]$ and simulate the reversal to check if the resulting string is lexicographically smaller than the original. While computationally expensive ($O(n^3)$ or $O(n^2)$ depending on implementation), this brute-force method guarantees finding a solution if one exists, though it may be too slow for $n=3 \\cdot 10^5$.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588391.0352218
      },
      "rev_n_3_efd60b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_efd60b_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_efd60b",
        "thought_text": "Identify the longest non-decreasing prefix of the string. Once the prefix ends at index $i$ (where $s[i] > s[i+1]$), the problem reduces to finding the first descent. If the entire string is non-decreasing, no such substring exists. This is essentially a refined version of the first strategy, focusing on the structural property of the string.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588391.0352294
      },
      "rev_n_4_930408_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_930408_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_930408",
        "thought_text": "Scan the string from left to right to find the first index i where s[i] > s[i+1]. Reversing the substring from i to i+1 will swap these two characters, making the new string lexicographically smaller at the first differing position. This is the most direct and optimal greedy approach.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1505210163641170512",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588433.3123777
      },
      "rev_n_5_88c18c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_88c18c_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_88c18c",
        "thought_text": "Identify the longest non-decreasing prefix of the string. Once the prefix ends at index k (where s[k] <= s[k+1] is false), the first inversion must occur at or after k. Specifically, finding the first i such that s[i] > s[i+1] is equivalent to finding the end of the longest non-decreasing prefix and checking the next pair.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1505210163641170512",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588433.3123922
      },
      "rev_n_6_9d1a9f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_9d1a9f_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_9d1a9f",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible substrings, reverse them, and compare the resulting string with the original to find any valid pair (l, r). While computationally expensive (O(n^3) or O(n^2) depending on implementation), this brute-force method guarantees finding a solution if one exists without relying on specific structural properties of the string.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1505210163641170512",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588433.3124003
      },
      "rev_n_7_5e7e40_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_5e7e40_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_5e7e40",
        "thought_text": "Scan the string from left to right to find the first index i where s[i] > s[i+1]. Reversing the substring from i to i+1 will swap these two characters, making the new string lexicographically smaller at the first differing position. This is the most direct and optimal greedy approach.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4140793779954324245",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588452.248238
      },
      "rev_n_8_07845c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_07845c_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_07845c",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through the string to find the longest non-increasing suffix starting from some index k. If such a suffix exists and is not the entire string, reversing the prefix ending just before this suffix might work, but the simple adjacent swap is sufficient and simpler to implement correctly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4140793779954324245",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588452.2482555
      },
      "rev_n_9_d1a605_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_d1a605_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_d1a605",
        "thought_text": "Check if the string is already sorted in non-decreasing order. If it is, no such substring exists, output NO. Otherwise, the first descent guarantees a solution by swapping the adjacent elements involved in the descent.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4140793779954324245",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588452.2482634
      },
      "rev_n_10_666f59_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_666f59_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_666f59",
        "thought_text": "Scan the string from left to right to find the first index $i$ where $s[i] > s[i+1]$. Reversing the substring from $i$ to $i+1$ (length 2) will swap these two characters, making the string lexicographically smaller at the earliest possible position, which is sufficient to satisfy the condition.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "3367660439368024271",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588469.1171353
      },
      "rev_n_11_1f175e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_1f175e_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_1f175e",
        "thought_text": "Identify the longest non-decreasing prefix of the string. Once the prefix ends at index $k$ (where $s[k] > s[k+1]$), any reversal starting at or before $k$ and ending after $k$ could work, but the simplest valid move is to reverse the specific pair $(k, k+1)$ where the first descent occurs.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "3367660439368024271",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588469.1171541
      },
      "rev_n_12_e313e2_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_e313e2_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_e313e2",
        "thought_text": "If the string is sorted in non-decreasing order (e.g., 'aabcfg'), no single reversal can make it lexicographically smaller because reversing any substring $s[l..r]$ where $s[l] eq s[r]$ would place a larger character earlier or keep the order same, failing the condition. Thus, check if the string is sorted; if so, output NO, otherwise find the first descent and output YES with the corresponding indices.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "3367660439368024271",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588469.1171618
      },
      "rev_n_13_1926de_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_1926de_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_1926de",
        "thought_text": "Scan the string from left to right to find the first index $i$ where $s[i] > s[i+1]$. If such an index exists, reversing the substring from $i+1$ to $i+2$ (1-based) will swap these two characters, making the string lexicographically smaller at the first point of difference. If no such index exists (the string is non-decreasing), output NO.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8412054547025486182",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588484.0747998
      },
      "rev_n_14_32dff6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_32dff6_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_32dff6",
        "thought_text": "Identify the longest prefix of the string that is sorted in non-decreasing order. Let this prefix end at index $k$. If $k < n$, then $s[k] > s[k+1]$. Reversing the substring starting at $k+1$ and ending at $k+2$ is a valid solution. This is logically equivalent to the first strategy but framed around the concept of a 'sorted prefix'.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8412054547025486182",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794588484.074816
      },
      "rev_n_15_a4cd25_0": {
